From 0000000000000000000000000000000000000000 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: DannyBaanks <danny@localhost>
Date: Mon, 9 Nov 2026 13:20:58 -0600
Subject: [PATCH] Linux: two runtimes, qemu and native, and an explicit guest
 frontier

ARCH=aarch64 (the default) and ARCH=x86_64 both build; each writes to gen/lin-<ARCH>/ and
dmhost_linux-<ARCH>, so one never overwrites the other. dm/host/run-linux runs either:
--mode qemu runs the ARM64 host under qemu-aarch64-static (or directly on an ARM64
machine), --mode native runs this machine's own host with no emulator on the path and no
fallback to one. Its exit codes name their own failures: 3 the emulator is missing, 4 the
machine has no native binary (or the binary is an ELF for another architecture), 5 the
binary was never built.

The x86_64 host is a host, not an emulator. It loads, relocates and inspects the game's
ARM64 modules and answers every Horizon service that does not need to execute them; the
modules' code cannot run, and the host says so first in the log and then at the boundary
with exit status 86 (main.c guest_exec_supported). There is no instruction-set translation
here and none was added: the game's own code runs natively or not at all.

What the x86_64 build needed, given that:

  linux/guest_stub_x86_64.c   the ARM64 assembly the guest enters (the gate stubs, the NVN
                              counting thunks, the thread entry, the big-stack entry) is
                              ARM64 instructions; the stub defines those symbols at the
                              sizes the host declares, and every entry reports and stops.
  asm_elf.py --names          the NVN name table as C, taken from the thunks file, so the
                              device resolves its 487 implementations by name identically
                              on both architectures (a blank table would have died).
  dm_ucontext.h / linux_compat.c   the register view of a Linux signal handler on x86_64
                              reads Intel's gregs (RIP, RSP, RBP, flags) and says there is
                              no link register and no NEON state, which the crash dump
                              then reports truthfully.
  hle.h, hle_bigstack.c, hle_offline.c, loader.c   the four places the guest's calling
                              convention was written down (the take-over stub, the stack
                              switch, the hidden x8 result, the tpidrro_el0 check) drop out
                              on x86_64, where there is no guest to apply it to.
  linux/linux_media_stub.c    the blank native images GameChat uses are anonymous storage,
                              not guest code, so they exist on x86_64 too.

The compat layer's test passes on both architectures, and linux/tests/adversarial_linux.sh
covers the ways the run can be asked for the wrong thing: no emulator, an ELF for another
architecture, a binary that was never built, a profile that does not parse.
Upstream-Status: fork-only (the Mac is the upstream platform; a Linux build is this fork's work)
Reason: One source tree, two hosts: the engine's portable C builds for ARM64 (where the guest's own code runs natively) and for x86_64 (where the host runs, loads and relocates the game's modules, and says where its code cannot). The guest-facing ARM64 assembly is not translated; the x86_64 build defines those symbols at the sizes the host declares and reports when one is reached, so the frontier is explicit instead of silent.
Issue: LNPANDY/NX2Apple#214

